<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Volturno</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Volturno is the longest river of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, about 175 km long, rising in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/molise">Molise</a> and flowing through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/campania">Campania</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyrrhenian-sea">Tyrrhenian Sea</a>. Ancient Capua and its medieval successor stood near its banks. In 1860 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giuseppe-garibaldi">Garibaldi</a> defeated the Neapolitan army on the river, and in 1943 it formed a German defensive line against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/volturno.jpg" alt="Volturno" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ermanon · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Volturno_017.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
